Now ContentInitializerTest — the LogManager static mock. With Mockito 5:

```java
@BeforeEach
public void setUp(){
    logManagerMock = Mockito.mockStatic(LogManager.class);
    logManagerMock.when(() -> LogManager.getLogger(any(Class.class))).thenReturn(loggerMock);
    contentInitializer = new ContentInitializer();
}

@AfterEach
public void tearDown() {
    logManagerMock.close();
}
```

Hmm — mocking log4j2's LogManager statically is risky: while the static mock is active, ANY code calling LogManager.getLogger breaks (returns mock or unstubbed null). Within these tests only ContentInitializer calls getLogger. BUT — other classes' static Logger fields initialize at CLASS LOAD time — e.g. if some class gets loaded during the test and its static Logger initializes via LogManager.getLogger → gets loggerMock (harmless) or null for unstubbed overload (e.g. getLogger(String) → mockStatic default returns null → NPE if used at class-init! e.g. `private static final Logger LOG = getLogger(Foo.class)` at class load of Foo → returns loggerMock (stubbed for Class param ✓)). getLogger(String) unstubbed → returns null → NPE in static init of classes using getLogger(String)... During these tests, which classes load? ContentInitializer + User/InterceptorRule models + InitializationService + log4j internals maybe. Models use `getLogger(X.class)`?? PersistentObject doesn't have a Logger. ContentInitializer uses `protected final Logger logger = getLogger(getClass());` — getClass() is a Class ✓ stubbed.

Also — mockStatic(LogManager.class) with Mockito: LogManager is a normal class (not final?). org.apache.logging.log4j.LogManager is a public non-final class with static methods — mockStatic instruments it ✓. But log4j2 itself might call LogManager.getLogger during the test window from its own internals (e.g. StatusLogger)? StatusLogger doesn't use LogManager. OK, feasible.

Alternative — simpler and less invasive: don't mock LogManager at all; instead replace the verify on loggerMock with... no — the test VERIFIES `loggerMock.info("Not initializing anything for SHOGun.")`. I need the ContentInitializer's logger to be the mock. The logger field is `protected final Logger logger = getLogger(getClass());` — I could use ReflectionTestUtils.setField to replace the logger instance after construction... but the field is final — ReflectionTestUtils CAN write final fields? It uses ReflectionUtils.setField → field.set — final instance fields writable after setAccessible... Actually Spring's ReflectionTestUtils.setField handles makeAccessible+set; on Java 17, setting final instance field of a normal class via reflection works (same as IdHelper). Hmm — but is that "the same test migrated"? The checker's migration is THEIRS; mine just needs to pass and be faithful. Mocking LogManager statically is the closest to the original PowerMock behavior. But it's fragile.

Cleaner + robust: 
```java
contentInitializer = new ContentInitializer();
ReflectionTestUtils.setField(contentInitializer, "logger", loggerMock);
```
Hmm — but field name is "logger" declared IN ContentInitializer (protected final Logger logger). ReflectionTestUtils.setField(target, "logger", value) — traverses class hierarchy ✓. Final field setting: ReflectionTestUtils → ReflectionUtils.makeAccessible(field) → field.set — Java 17: setting final instance fields via reflection works for non-record, non-hidden classes (it's what Hibernate does). BUT — there's a wrinkle: `Field.set` on a FINAL field requires `setAccessible(true)` to succeed AND (since JDK 9?) — actually JDK 17: `field.setAccessible(true); field.set(obj, newValue);` on a final INSTANCE field — works (the "final" check is in Field.setAccessor... the rule: if the field is final, Field.set throws IllegalAccessException UNLESS the field came from the same class... no. The actual rule in JDK 17: Field.set on final instance field works if setAccessible(true) succeeded. For static final it always throws. ✓ Works.

Hmm — but wait: does the ORIGINAL test behavior differ? Original: loggerMock injected via LogManager mock — the logger field got the mock AT CONSTRUCTION. Then verify(loggerMock).info(...) checks the "Not initializing anything" path. With setField approach — same end state (logger field = mock). ✓ 

But hold on — for the OTHER two tests (callsSaveUserIfPersistentObjectIsAnUser), the logger mock isn't really verified — only initializationService. Both approaches work.

Actually — even simpler: keep mockStatic but scope it tightly in setUp/@AfterEach. I'll go with the mockStatic approach since it's most faithful and works on Java 17 with Mockito 5 inline mockmaker. Hmm — one concern: mockStatic(LogManager) might conflict with log4j's initialization for the test framework — surefire logging happens before tests. The mock is active only during each test method. Fine.

Hmm, wait — actually there's one more risk with mockStatic(LogManager.class): Mockito's inline mockmaker instruments LogManager via bytecode transformation. Any threads calling it concurrently would break. GreenMail/mail tests are separate classes. This test: single-threaded. OK.

Decision: mockStatic in @BeforeEach, close in @AfterEach. Let me write it.

