The original test uses plain `MockMvcBuilders.standaloneSetup(applicationController).build()`. In Spring 5.3, how did joda DateTime serialize? 

OH WAIT — I bet in Spring 5.3, the StandaloneMockMvcBuilder's default Jackson converter ObjectMapper DOES register modules found by SERVICE LOADER? In Spring 5.3's `StandaloneMockMvcBuilder.getMessageConverters` → default includes `new MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter()` — plain. Hmm...

Hmm, let me think about Jackson version differences: jackson 2.14 (original) vs 2.15 (mine). Both would fail on joda DateTime without JodaModule: "Joda date/time type `org.joda.time.DateTime` not supported by default: add Module jackson-datatype-joda". This message appears in Jackson 2.10+. In jackson 2.14 the message is the same. So the ORIGINAL must have had the JodaModule registered somehow...

OH!!! I know!!! **`ObjectMapper.findModules()`** — `MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter()` default ctor in SPRING 6.0+: Let me check the actual spring-web 6.1.15 code: `MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter()` → `this(Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder.json().build())` — YES! In Spring 5.x and 6.x, MappingJackson2HttpMessageConverter's default constructor uses Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder.json().build() — and **Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder registers well-known modules**: let me recall its `registerWellKnownModulesIfAvailable()`: it tries to register: jackson-module-kotlin, jackson-datatype-jdk8, jackson-datatype-jsr310, **jackson-datatype-joda**?? Hmm — actually I DO recall joda being in that list! Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder:

```java
private void registerWellKnownModulesIfAvailable(ObjectMapper objectMapper) {
    // register well-known modules if available on classpath
    ... kotlin
    ... jdk8 (Java8)
    ... java.time (JSR-310)
    ... joda (only if Java8 not present??)  ← hmm!!
    ... jaxb
}
```

YES!! There it is: `jackson-datatype-joda` IS in the well-known list, BUT — **it's registered ONLY when `java.time` (JSR-310) is NOT available**: the logic in Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder:

```java
try {
    // Java 8 java.time support
    Class javaTimeModule = ClassUtils.forName("com.fasterxml.jackson.datatype.jsr310.JavaTimeModule", ...);
    ... register JavaTimeModule
}
catch ...
try {
    // Joda-Time support
    Class jodaModule = ClassUtils.forName("com.fasterxml.jackson.datatype.joda.JodaModule", ...);
    ... register JodaModule
}
```
Hmm — both registered unconditionally? I remember there being an interplay... Actually I'm quite sure both are attempted independently. Then in Spring 5.3 + our classpath (jackson-datatype-joda 2.14.3 present!) → JodaModule registered by the builder → serialization worked!

In MY run: jackson-datatype-joda 2.15.4 IS on the test classpath ✓... so why the failure?! "Joda date/time type org.joda.time.DateTime not supported by default: add Module jackson-datatype-joda" — means the ObjectMapper didn't have JodaModule. So in Spring 6.1.15's Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder, joda registration is... Let me check the actual spring-web 6.1.15 jar's Jackson2ObjectMapperBuilder for the well-known modules list. Let me decompile/strings it.

